<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhuangzi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 369–286 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Zhuang Zhou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhuangzi (Zhuang Zhou) was a Daoist philosopher of the 4th century BCE, a native of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. The book named after him, with the Laozi a founding text of Daoism, uses parable and paradox to question fixed values and celebrate spontaneity, as in his dream of being a butterfly. It strongly shaped later Chinese literature, the 'pure conversation' of the Jin aristocracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Chan Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhuangzi.jpg" alt="Zhuangzi" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hua Zili (華祖立) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%84%E9%96%80%E5%8D%81%E5%AD%90%E5%9C%96_%E8%8E%8A%E5%AD%90.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
